data.content:
Here's what happened and how to fix it:

## The Problem

In C# 14 / .NET 10, new `Span<T>` overload resolution rules cause `array.Contains(num)` to bind to **`MemoryExtensions.Contains<T>(Span<T>, T)`** instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`. 

The `MemoryExtensions.Contains` overload is **not representable in LINQ expression trees**, so it throws at runtime (expression trees don't support span-based methods).

## The Fix

Force the compiler to pick the `Enumerable` overload by casting the array to `IEnumerable<int>`:

```csharp
// Option 1: .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 2: explicit cast
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 3: explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

All three prevent the span overload from being selected and keep the expression tree valid.
